      "problem": "If ${4}^{x}=\\frac{1}{4},$ then $x=${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{4}$ as ${4}^{-1}$ to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{x} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {4}^{x} & ={4}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(4)$, we equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{x} & ={4}^{-1} \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1.$",
